-- Passage rg6-comparing-texts-p05
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('40f85cff-969a-5d21-a066-37d40a119aea', 'rg6-comparing-texts-p05', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g6-comparing-texts'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Across the Miles$q$, $q$Text 1: Sunday Chess (a story)

Every Sunday at nine in the morning, Rafael set up his chessboard in front of the laptop. Six thousand miles away, it was already evening, and his grandfather Abuelo Ernesto set up his own board.

"Knight to f3," Rafael said. On the screen, Abuelo moved his knight on his board. Sometimes the video froze for a second, and Abuelo's face stopped with his mouth wide open. Rafael always laughed.

They never finished a game in one call. Abuelo talked about his tomato plants. Rafael talked about his science project. Between moves, they told each other about the week.

One Sunday, the internet went out. Rafael sat in front of the dark screen and felt strangely empty. He took a photo of the board so he would remember where every piece stood. The next week, they picked up the game exactly where they had left off.

Text 2: The Letter Box (a poem)

In 1921, my great-grandmother wrote:
The ship took forty days.
She sealed the paper in an envelope
and sent her words away.

It crossed the sea in a canvas sack.
It waited in a town.
For weeks her sister checked the box,
then finally sat down

and read about the city streets,
the rain, the work, the cold.
She wrote back in her careful hand
each time the year grew old.

The letters still sit in a tin.
The ink has faded gray.
But every page still says to me:
I did not go away.$q$, 'draft')
